        "label": "planned to visit",
        "verses": ["13"],
        "references": [
          "Acts 19:21 - I must see Rome",
          "Acts 20:1-3 - Paul probably wrote Romans during his 3 month stay near Corinth",
          "Romans 16:1 - Cenchreae is near Corinth",
          "Romans 16:23 - Erastus likely city treasurer of Corinth"
        ],
        "emphasis": ["planned", "prevented"],
        "note": [
          "Claim: Paul likely wrote Romans during his 3 month stay near Corinth (Acts 2:3)",
          "Supporting evidence: Cenchrea mentioned in Romans 16:1 is 6 miles from Corinth (see map linked under references on this button).",
          "Supporting evidence: Erastus mentioned in Romans 16:23 was likely a city official in Corinth",
          "In 1929, excavators from the American School of Classical Studies at Athens found a limestone paving slab in situ in the paved plaza east of the theatre at ancient Corinth. The Latin text reads: ",
          "ERASTVS PRO AEDILIT[AT]E S[UA] P[ECUNIA] STRAVIT \"Erastus, in return for his aedileship, laid this pavement at his own expense.\"",
          "Note that the letters in brackets are restored, not actually preserved — the bronze inlay letters are gone, leaving only the cuttings. The standard publication is Corinth VIII.3, no. 232 (John Harvey Kent, 1966); it's also catalogued as AE 1930, 118 and EDH HD025815. The slab is reportedly still visible in situ at the site.",
          "This provides remarkable archaeological evidence supporting the historical existence of Erastus mentioned in Romans 16:23, but obviously not proof it was the same individual."
        ],
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Corinth is between Greece and Achaia. Cenchrea is nearby.",
